          "body": "## Scope\n\nCloses #358. Independent follow-up to #357; no package fixes and no previous PR merged.\n\nStart with `research/contracts/README.md`. `SPEC.md` states the target contract. The models, input check, custom SQLAlchemy types, literal migrations, runner, and evidence are beside it.\n\n## Findings\n\n- Both tailored implementations matched the expected accept/reject outcomes for 64 application cases across SQLite and MariaDB. Writes use full snekql transactions or SQLAlchemy AsyncSession flush/commit, followed by independent readback.\n- SQLAlchemy custom types repair the earlier UTC mismatch. Attribute validation, bind validation, connection policy, and bulk omission behavior all require explicit decisions.\n- Bulk timestamp None initially received a server default until evaluates_none() made the custom binder reject it. The focused test caught this before the example was corrected.\n- snekql deliberately normalizes bool to int before field validation. A separate input boundary is needed for our stronger actual-int-only contract. The native behavior remains recorded separately.\n- snekql literal defaults work with migration SQL and GenCol/PENDING_GENERATION, but strict schema verification rejects the intended default. Removing CHECKs adds no verification issue; migration history remains valid after live schema corruption.\n- SQLAlchemy create_all neither repairs nor rejects the corresponding corrupted existing schemas. This study does not claim it replaces a verifier or migration system.\n\nThe report compares actual implementation and maintenance decisions, not usability scores. Strict verification remains an unmet deployment requirement; this is not a claim of complete equivalence.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Four isolated backend/library runs.\n- 64 application cases and 120 raw SQL observations across baseline and deliberately corrupted schemas, plus native-input and bulk-write diagnostics.\n- Full package plus research suite: 1356 passed, including 18 research checks.\n- Final focused tests, ty, Ruff lint, and Ruff formatting passed.\n- Extra comparator dependencies pinned separately. No uv.lock or production-code changes.\n- Root typing exception limited to SQLAlchemy mapped_column declarations in one research file.\n\nLimits include untested updates, corrupt-row materialization, migration upgrades, concurrency, and broader driver/backend versions. Temporary MariaDB binaries are required locally; the runner accepts no external database URL.\n",
